db/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1039 "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1045 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "44aff9fc-2508-51a9-baf1-3f855c1f0a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ is a sample of size $n=50$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be802189-e841-547b-9c11-133fd34a8081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157598 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =30,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=30.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1061 29343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fab3a24b-2709-54fe-985c-5fe95ec0712c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1061 29347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a21dbb27-5fb2-54ba-a76f-f7231e67b5fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1061 38136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4fadf35-c701-59e2-9c8b-d385cd3565ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1061 38137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d85b5ed-e218-547b-81cf-36a60c612b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1061 38142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2edb1e12-aed2-5310-a098-962a8588a4f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1\\. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n2\\. Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n3\\. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1065 4738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "359539bd-254d-5083-aa76-577b8a159438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1064 "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1065 6297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b79ae73d-8ebe-5dda-916d-6eeb61ce850d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1064 "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1065 6298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c9ca398-f024-59cf-b484-80cf2f43cc8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean as an Unbiased Estimator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172055 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172056 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1039 "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172132 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Surface of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172133 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1045 "Surfaces of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172185 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d69714f2-bb12-5119-931a-cf96944e88c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172933 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172933 #edb/ref 1061 33155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d490ab1-06f7-5e16-b643-b1b0d8fab4fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172934 #edb/ref 1061 33156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9771f588-30ca-519b-9cd3-5da25e6f18e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172935 #edb/ref 1061 33157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8409e7cf-c975-5a30-a407-4fc56e97763f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172965 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172965 #edb/ref 1061 34312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172965 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23271cc4-5b18-5ab7-87a0-420c4bf3d6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172965 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173083 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173083 #edb/ref 1061 44310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2109942c-c8ff-56d5-8311-ec29a24f7390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173084 #edb/ref 1061 44313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c37f4ed-7df7-5a6c-855e-263d81b3f5b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173085 #edb/ref 1061 44314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173085 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3533d6b6-1b8e-5af8-b6c0-195bca89c86d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173085 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173086 #edb/ref 1061 44408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93cfa70b-b133-5659-9bce-ab370759c49e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173087 #edb/ref 1061 44409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6100252f-8102-5700-8f5a-11a2b77335a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173089 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173089 #edb/ref 1061 44739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173089 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3100ec93-73c0-5fba-bebc-ee40c07ece7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173089 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173175 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2].$\n\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0,$ we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\n\n \nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2],$ these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad \nx \\in [0,2], \n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173175 #edb/ref 1065 5305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173175 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9bd13796-1bf7-5a1b-a9f4-87567fd6998c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173175 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173186 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve $x=x(y)\\geq 0$ for $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y.$\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[ \n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve $z=z(y)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique.\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173186 #edb/ref 1065 5507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173186 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae882ec8-1ebd-5277-8224-b440b433c8ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173186 #edb/ref 1067 "Formulas for Rotation About the y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173253 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution.\n\nSuppose that the curve [math]y=f(x)[/math] for [math]x \\in [a,b][/math] is rotated [math]2\\pi[/math] radians about the [math]x[/math]-axis. The corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad (x,\\theta) \\in D, \n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]D= \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: x \\in[a,b], \\: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi) \\big\\}.[/math] How can we find the area of this surface?\n\nFirst, we now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 1, \\:f'(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 0, \\: -f(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\\n0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta\n\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\bigg\\langle \\begin{vmatrix} f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ 0  & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta \\\\ 0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta  \\end{vmatrix} \\bigg\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x)\\cos^2\\theta+f(x)f'(x)\\sin^2\\theta, \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x), \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\:- f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nand\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|\n&= \\sqrt{\\big( f(x)f'(x) \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 \\big( \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\big) } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 }.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA \n&= \\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|  \\text{d}x\\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}\\theta \\: \\text{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\big[ \\theta \\big]_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\pi \\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}x.\n\\end{align*}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173253 #edb/ref 1065 7638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173253 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "79f42de8-4cc4-576a-b367-c08bd87f8bcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173253 #edb/ref 1067 "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173254 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x.$\n\nIn general, given a curve $y=y(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173254 #edb/ref 1065 7648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173254 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3148fb53-9ae7-5747-aa63-b79dd1bc224f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173254 #edb/ref 1067 "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating y=y(x) About the x-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173255 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the point $(x(z),0,z)$ on the curve $x=x(z)\\geq 0$ for $z\\in [a,b]$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50473a5407143c7ec49ce8c192c256bbc184703010209767507e4b922e9f4c7b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: x(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFinally, the rotation of a point $(0,y(z),z)$ on a curve $y=y(z)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $z$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5ff1574db4d105e7d69cc12ed609898d626b4977902f98dbcbcac6633ad56d2b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle y(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173255 #edb/ref 1065 7649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173255 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e8a0947-f30a-5da6-9c3b-7b93bdba56bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173255 #edb/ref 1067 "Formulas for Rotation About the z-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173257 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the point $(x,0,z(x))$ that lies on a curve $z=z(x)$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74686e9e17ce056fea730b47bdf34ee582cc83920200f0345f75b90a6f8c9833.png)\n\nIn general, given a curve $z=z(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAgain, this parametrization is not unique. An alternative parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173257 #edb/ref 1065 7714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173257 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c06437e5-d847-5d03-aac0-497649901444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173257 #edb/ref 1067 "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating z=z(x) About the x-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179731 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861